# Control.js

This document describes [Control.js]() with emphasis on the rendering process of `vcl/Control` and its derived classes.

The central observation of this thread is that `initializeNodes()` and `render()` are two adjacent lifecycle phases with overlapping practical responsibilities. `initializeNodes()` already acts as the moment where `this._node` and `this._nodes` become usable for subclass logic, while `render()` remains the broader invalidation-driven projection phase. The purpose of this document is to make that split explicit, document the current patterns in the framework, and create a structure that can later grow into a much larger `Control.js.smdl`.

## Functionality

`Control.js` is the central visual lifecycle module of the framework. It owns the transition from abstract component state into concrete DOM state, exposes the base rendering contract, participates in state reflection through `_update()`, and provides the structural hooks that most visual subclasses build on top of.

Functionally, `Control.js` is where the following concerns meet:

- node creation and destruction
- stateful visibility and invalidation
- DOM access through `this._node` and `this._nodes`
- render dispatch through `render()` and `onRender`
- the queue-driven update cycle through `update()` and `_update()`

## Position In The Framework

`Control.js` sits between the generic component model and the UI class hierarchy.

- Above it sits `Component.js`, which provides properties, ownership, event dispatch and general component lifecycle.
- Below it sit visual controls such as `Element`, `Panel`, `Input`, `Node`, `Tab`, `List`, and many specializations built on top of those.
- Beside it sits `ControlUpdater.js`, which turns invalidation into queued update work.

Because of that position, `Control.js` does not define one concrete rendering strategy. Instead, it defines the base visual lifecycle that descendants may preserve, extend, or replace.

## Lifecycle Overview

The current `vcl/Control` rendering path is not one single method. It is a chain of phases.

### Core flow

1. A node is created in `createNode()`.
2. `this._node` and `this._nodes` become available.
3. `initializeNodes()` performs initial node wiring.
4. `nodecreated` is dispatched.
5. `render()` is called once immediately after node creation.
6. Later visual and state changes flow through `update()` and `_update()`.
7. When the control is marked `invalidated`, `_update()` calls `render()` again.

### Important consequence

`render()` is not the only place where DOM-facing work happens. In practice, `initializeNodes()` is already a distinct lifecycle phase that many controls use for node lookup, child-node binding, event hookup and early DOM preparation.

That makes `initializeNodes()` more than just a small helper. It is already a structural counterpart to `render()`.

## Relevant Source Anchors

- [Control.js](./Control.js)
- `createNode()` around the node creation and first render
- `initializeNodes()` in the base class
- `render()` in the base class
- `_update()` where `invalidated` leads back to `render()`

## `createNode()`

`createNode()` is the place where the control crosses from abstract component state into concrete DOM state.

### Current sequence

```js
createNode: function(documentNode) {
	this._node = documentNode.createElement(this.getElement());
	this._node.id = this.getNodeId();
	this._node.innerHTML = this.getInnerHtml();
	if(this._tabIndex !== -1) {
		this._node.tabIndex = this._tabIndex;
	}
	this._nodes = {};
	this.initializeNodes();
	this.dispatch("nodecreated", this._node, this._nodes);
	this.render();
}
```

### Observations

- `this._node` exists before `initializeNodes()` is entered.
- `this._nodes` exists before `initializeNodes()` is entered.
- `initializeNodes()` runs before the first `render()`.
- `render()` therefore already assumes node lookup and node registration are done.

This ordering is the key to understanding why `initializeNodes()` is a viable place for framework-level rendering cleanup.

## `initializeNodes()`

### Base implementation

The base `initializeNodes()` is deliberately small:

```js
initializeNodes: function() {
	this._node.onscroll = EventDispatcher.handleEvent;
	// FIXME there are more events that might need to be hooked
},
```

At first glance this looks incidental, but its position in the lifecycle gives it a much larger meaning than the implementation suggests.

### Semantic role

`initializeNodes()` is the phase where:

- the DOM node already exists
- named child-node references can be stored in `this._nodes`
- structural DOM relationships can be established
- node-level event handlers can be connected
- one-time-per-node setup can happen before the first render pass

### Why it matters

A recurring problem in the current framework is that many `render()` overrides are forced to do work that is not really repeatable rendering. They often contain node lookup, child registration or structural DOM maintenance, simply because those operations require access to `this._node` and `this._nodes`.

`initializeNodes()` already solves that access problem cleanly.

### Not one-time per control

`initializeNodes()` is not a one-time hook for the life of the component. It is a one-time hook per node instance.

That matters because:

- `recreateNode()` destroys and rebuilds the DOM node
- any future extension point in this phase must be idempotent
- node-level setup must tolerate being applied again after recreation

### Practical reading

The best way to read `initializeNodes()` is:

> "The node exists now; bind your DOM references and perform structural setup that should happen before the first and every subsequent render pass for this node instance."

### Relation to the current thread

This is exactly why `initializeNodes()` can help "fix" the ambiguity of `render()` without forcing `render()` to become narrowly about low-level DOM painting.

`initializeNodes()` can absorb:

- node registration
- child-node discovery
- DOM event hookup
- structural node augmentation

while `render()` can remain the repeatable projection/update phase.

## `render()`

### Base implementation

The base implementation is very small and intentionally abstract:

```js
render: function() {
	if(this.isVisible()) {
		return this.fire("onRender", arguments);
	} else {
		this.setState("invalidated");
	}
},
```

### What the base contract really says

The base class does not define rendering as "apply these DOM changes". Instead it defines rendering as:

- only render while visible
- otherwise remain invalidated
- delegate actual rendering behavior to the `onRender` event

This is important. In `vcl/Control`, `render()` is less a built-in paint routine and more a visibility-gated render dispatch.

### The strengths of this design

- `render()` stays abstract
- prototype resources can implement rendering through `onRender`
- controls without custom DOM projection do not need a large built-in render body
- the base class does not force a single visual model onto all descendants

### The costs of this design

- subclasses are free to redefine `render()` completely
- some subclasses do not call inherited `render()`
- `onRender` is only meaningful for some controls
- "render" can mean dispatch, DOM sync, structural patching, or all three

### `onRender`

`onRender` is a `Type.EVENT` property backed by `_onRender`. It is dispatched by `fire()`, which means:

- the event is suppressed while loading
- the event is suppressed while designing
- the return value comes directly from the `_onRender` function

That suppression behavior is consistent with normal event semantics, but not always with lifecycle semantics. This is one of the reasons a future `initializeNodes`-adjacent hook may need different dispatch rules.

## `update()` and `_update()`

`render()` is called in two main ways:

- immediately from `createNode()`
- later from `_update()` when the control is `invalidated`

### Why `_update()` matters

The real visual lifecycle is state-driven:

- setters mark a control invalidated
- `update()` queues the control
- `ControlUpdater` eventually runs `_update()`
- `_update()` reflects control state and calls `render()` when needed

This means the practical rendering model is:

> invalidation + visibility + queued update + render dispatch

That model is broader than simple DOM repainting, which again supports keeping `render()` abstract.

## `initializeNodes()` vs `render()`

The cleanest separation is not "setup vs render" in the generic sense, but this:

### `initializeNodes()`

- per-node lifecycle phase
- has access to `this._node`
- has access to `this._nodes`
- registers child refs
- wires node-level events
- performs structural setup
- should be idempotent across node recreation

### `render()`

- repeatable lifecycle phase
- gated by visibility in the base class
- driven by invalidation and update queueing
- projects component state into the current node
- may remain abstract or domain-specific

### Working distinction

If an operation depends on the node existing but does not need to run on every invalidation, it is a strong candidate for `initializeNodes()`.

If an operation reflects current component state and may need to run again after property changes, it belongs in `render()` or in methods that `render()` calls.

## Derived Class Patterns

The framework currently uses several different rendering patterns. This section groups them by behavior.

### Pattern A: additive `initializeNodes()`, base-style render dispatch

These classes extend `initializeNodes()` and preserve the base render idea.

Examples:

- [ui/Panel.js](./ui/Panel.js)
- many `Element`-based prototype resources using `onRender`

Characteristics:

- `initializeNodes()` adds node wiring
- `render()` still delegates to inherited behavior, or allows `onRender`
- `onRender` remains meaningful

### Pattern B: additive `initializeNodes()`, additive `render()`

These classes do their own DOM sync and then still call inherited `render()`.

Examples:

- [ui/Node.js](./ui/Node.js)
- [ui/Panel.js](./ui/Panel.js)
- [ui/Node.closeable.js](./ui/Node.closeable.js)

Characteristics:

- the class has its own built-in render work
- `onRender` may still run
- ordering differs per class

Notes:

- `Node` renders text and icon, then calls inherited `render()`
- `Panel` applies zoom, then calls inherited `render()`
- `Node.closeable` calls inherited `render()` first, then mutates close-button structure

These are already three different sub-variants inside the same broad pattern.

### Pattern C: full render replacement

These classes override `render()` and never call inherited `render()`.

Examples:

- [ui/Input.js](./ui/Input.js)
- [ui/Select.js](./ui/Select.js)
- [ui/Checkbox.js](./ui/Checkbox.js)
- [ui/Radiobutton.js](./ui/Radiobutton.js)
- [ui/CheckGroup.js](./ui/CheckGroup.js)

Characteristics:

- `render()` means "synchronize DOM state now"
- `onRender` is effectively bypassed
- visibility gating from the base class is bypassed

This is one of the main reasons the meaning of `render()` is currently inconsistent across control types.

### Pattern D: render interception and short-circuit

The most notable example is [ui/Tab.js](./ui/Tab.js).

Its render method behaves like this:

- if no `_onRender` exists, perform default text rendering
- if `_onRender` exists and returns `false`, still perform default text rendering
- otherwise suppress default rendering

This is a real "event overrides default rendering" pattern, but it is unique enough that it should be treated as a special case rather than the framework norm.

### Pattern E: structural rendering in `initializeNodes()`

The list subsystem already demonstrates that `initializeNodes()` can carry structural render responsibility.

Examples:

- [ui/List.js](./ui/List.js)
- [ui/ListRow.js](./ui/ListRow.js)

Characteristics:

- child node references are bound in `initializeNodes()`
- structural HTML may be refreshed there
- cells may be rendered during node initialization

This is important because it proves the framework already tolerates a broader interpretation of `initializeNodes()` than the base implementation suggests.

## Representative Derived Classes

### `vcl/ui/Input`

`initializeNodes()`:

- ensures `this._nodes.input` exists
- applies placeholder, type and name
- hooks native input events

`render()`:

- reads either `_value` or the bound source attribute
- writes the result into the input node

Interpretation:

- `initializeNodes()` handles node preparation
- `render()` performs repeatable value projection

This is actually a fairly good split, even though it bypasses inherited `render()`.

### `vcl/ui/Checkbox` and `vcl/ui/Radiobutton`

`initializeNodes()`:

- finds `input` and `label`
- wires `for` to the generated input id

`render()`:

- updates checked state
- updates label HTML

Interpretation:

- these are strong candidates for the "node setup in `initializeNodes()`, state projection in `render()`" mental model
- they still bypass `onRender`

### `vcl/ui/Select`

`initializeNodes()`:

- hooks native events

`render()`:

- rebuilds the `<option>` list
- chooses a selected value

Interpretation:

- this mixes structural DOM rebuilding with state projection inside `render()`
- a future refactor might separate "option structure changed" from "selected value changed"

### `vcl/ui/Node`

`initializeNodes()`:

- binds `selection`, `icon`, `text` and `container`
- validates required child nodes

`render()`:

- optionally lets the tree intercept node rendering
- applies text and icon state
- then calls inherited `render()`

Interpretation:

- this is a layered lifecycle
- the tree-level `onNodeRender` hook is the real render override point, not `onRender`

### `vcl/ui/Tab`

`initializeNodes()`:

- binds text and close nodes

`render()`:

- uses `_onRender` as an override gate for built-in text rendering

Interpretation:

- `Tab` is the clearest example of `onRender` overriding a control's default rendering
- the return-value convention is unusual and easy to misread

### `vcl/ui/Panel`

`initializeNodes()`:

- adds focusability concerns

`render()`:

- applies zoom styling
- then calls inherited `render()`

Interpretation:

- this is an additive render model
- built-in DOM sync and `onRender` coexist

### `vcl/ui/List` and `vcl/ui/ListRow`

`initializeNodes()` in these classes does more than plain child-node registration.

It also:

- binds structural nodes
- ensures inner HTML matches expectations
- performs cell rendering during node setup

Interpretation:

- these classes demonstrate that `initializeNodes()` can already function as a structural rendering phase
- they strengthen the case for a first-class `initializeNodes` lifecycle hook

## Inconsistencies

The current framework does not have one consistent meaning of `render()`.

### 1. Event dispatch vs DOM sync

In the base class, `render()` means "fire `onRender` when visible".

In many descendants, `render()` means "synchronize DOM directly".

### 2. Visibility gating

The base class defers rendering while invisible by setting `invalidated` again.

Descendants that bypass inherited `render()` also bypass this behavior.

### 3. `onRender` availability

Some controls expose `onRender` as a meaningful lifecycle hook.

Others define it as a property but never route rendering through it.

### 4. Ordering differences

Some controls run built-in render logic before inherited render.

Some after.

Some never.

That means the same `onRender` event can be:

- a primary render implementation
- an after-render hook
- a pre-render veto point
- or dead code

depending on the control type.

### 5. Structural work in `render()`

Some controls rebuild node structure in `render()` even when the operation is not truly a repeatable state projection.

This often happens because no formal lifecycle hook exists between:

- "the node exists now"
- and
- "perform repeatable render work"

### 6. Loading and designing suppression

Because `onRender` runs through `fire()`, it is suppressed during loading and designing.

That behavior may be correct for user-facing events, but it is less obviously correct for lifecycle extension points.

## Why `initializeNodes()` Helps

This thread's hypothesis is:

> `initializeNodes()` is already a sort of render method, or at least a method that can absorb part of the brokenness currently concentrated in `render()`.

That makes sense for three reasons.

### Reason 1: correct timing

When `initializeNodes()` runs:

- the DOM node exists
- child lookup is possible
- node-local structure can be installed

This is exactly the moment many current `render()` workarounds need.

### Reason 2: clearer repeatability boundary

Moving structural setup into `initializeNodes()` makes `render()` easier to read as a repeatable phase.

### Reason 3: already proven in real subclasses

The list subsystem and several control types already treat `initializeNodes()` as more than a trivial helper.

So formalizing that role is not an alien design move; it is codifying an existing pattern.

## Candidate Extension Point

If the framework introduces a new property or event to steer `initializeNodes()`, its semantics should be explicit.

### Desired contract

The new hook would mean:

> "The node has been created and `this._nodes` is available. Perform node-level setup now."

### Requirements

- runs after `this._node` and `this._nodes` exist
- runs during every node creation and recreation
- does not assume this is the final render pass
- is safe for idempotent structural setup

### Open design question

If implemented as a normal `Type.EVENT` routed through `fire()`, the hook would be suppressed during loading and designing.

That may be undesirable for a lifecycle hook in this phase.

So this extension point probably needs one of these behaviors:

- a forced `fire(..., true)` style dispatch
- a direct `_onInitializeNodes` call
- a dedicated lifecycle dispatcher distinct from general events

## Direction

The most promising direction is not to narrow `render()` to pure DOM painting.

Instead:

- keep `render()` abstract and repeatable
- recognize `initializeNodes()` as a first-class lifecycle phase
- move structural DOM setup pressure out of `render()`
- make subclass behavior easier to compare against an explicit two-phase model

## Recommended Mental Model

For `vcl/Control` and its descendants, the most useful mental model today is:

### Phase 1: node creation

- create the element
- install inner HTML
- create `this._nodes`

### Phase 2: node initialization

- run `initializeNodes()`
- bind refs
- wire DOM events
- perform structural setup

### Phase 3: first render

- project current component state into the node
- optionally route through `onRender`

### Phase 4: queued rerender

- property changes invalidate the control
- `_update()` eventually runs
- `render()` runs again when visible

## Short Conclusion

`initializeNodes()` and `render()` are not competing concepts. They are two adjacent lifecycle phases that have been carrying overlapping responsibilities.

The framework becomes easier to reason about when:

- `initializeNodes()` owns node-availability concerns
- `render()` owns repeatable projection concerns
- subclass deviations are documented as explicit patterns instead of accidental differences

That is the basis on which this document should grow.
